{"attack": true, "case": "The target's proposed mechanism is internally contradictory regarding its stated goal. It claims that preferring the 'most robust foundation' (the highly corroborated theory) will 'maximize the rate of error detection' and accelerate falsification. However, high corroboration precisely means a theory has already survived numerous severe attempts at falsification, demonstrating its resistance to error detection. If the genuine goal is to accelerate falsification, selecting theories that have already proven resistant to it is self-defeating; to maximize the rate of error detection, one would prefer novel, untested, or less corroborated theories that are more likely to be quickly falsified. The target conflates the utility of a 'robust foundation' with the 'rate of falsification,' failing to recognize that highly corroborated theories historically create epistemic inertia that resists error detection.", "counterexample": ["Newtonian mechanics was a highly corroborated, 'robust foundation' for centuries, yet its very robustness resisted falsification and slowed the detection of its own errors (e.g., Mercury's perihelion) compared to alternative novel hypotheses."], "premise": "A highly corroborated theory, by definition, has already survived severe attempts at falsification, making it more resistant to future error detection than a novel, untested theory.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "0d04e6d020047340546246ee438e89a1aa539ac776b4900148b0267d28d13b4f", "quote": "It is not an inductive prediction of eternal survival, but a strategic choice to build upon the most robust foundation currently available to maximize the rate of error detection. Induction is not smuggled in because the goal is not truth-probability but the acceleration of falsification."}], "successor_question": "How can preferring the most highly corroborated theory—a theory that has already survived numerous severe tests—possibly maximize the rate of error detection, given that its very corroboration demonstrates its resistance to being falsified?"}